  "mathematics": {
    "scope": "Complete original Chapter19 recursive source read: three author inputs and329 lines. Earlier Chapter1,4,6,8,18 inputs retain their recorded current-cycle scopes; the explicitly cited SGPT almost-semigroup result, Riemann-Roch/Serre duality and Hartogs theorem remain classical inputs rather than newly independently reproved here. Chapter20 and26 pointers are transition references only.",
    "fresh_checks": [
      "Local multiplier-ideal membership gives global finite weighted norm by a finite compact covering, and the converse restricts to coordinate balls; the norm is positive definite on the finite-dimensional section subspace.",
      "All-weight ideal order gives inclusion at each integer degree; ordinary equivalence preserves section spaces but changes their Hilbert norms by bounded factors.",
      "The ratio exp(-m(v-u)+F) lies between exp(-mD-E) and exp(mD+E); both integrability equivalence and squared-norm bounds follow with the displayed signs.",
      "Unitary invariance of the orthonormal basis gives the same finite evaluation kernel; the N=0 convention remains zero, with potential identically minus infinity.",
      "Cauchy-Schwarz gives the evaluation extremum, achieved by the conjugate coefficient vector in a one-dimensional local fiber; both sides are zero at common base points.",
      "The pole set has smooth volume zero. Integrating the finite sum of normalized section densities gives exactly N_m, including the declared zero density on poles.",
      "The local kernel equals exp(-m chi) times a finite sum of squared holomorphic coefficients. A nonzero global section cannot vanish identically on a coordinate open set; thus its log/m defines a genuine analytic-type potential.",
      "The local curvature identity cancels chi and gives a positive closed dd^c log-sum/m current in the line-bundle curvature class.",
      "Applying the squared-norm bounds to the evaluation extremum yields the potential error D+E/m away from the common base locus and hence the same ordinary singularity type.",
      "The simultaneous change h to h exp(-a) and u to u-a preserves the weighted product exactly, changes beta to beta+dd^c a and Phi to Phi-a, and preserves both density and curvature current.",
      "Gauduchon degree is independent of the smooth line-bundle metric since pairing dd^c a with gamma^(n-1) vanishes by distributional Stokes.",
      "Poincare-Lelong for a nonzero section pairs to m deg_gamma L = integral_D gamma^(n-1). Every nonempty effective divisor gives a strictly positive pairing, including points in dimension one.",
      "Negative degree forbids all positive-degree sections. At zero degree any nonzero section has empty divisor and trivializes the power; compact connectedness makes global holomorphic functions constant and the full or nonzero weighted space one-dimensional.",
      "A positive curvature current of zero Gauduchon degree is zero since its positive trace measure against a strictly positive metric is zero; no top-degree nonlinear mass inference is used.",
      "Restricted sections belong to the image ideal on Y, while the full intrinsic ideal section space may contain nonextendable sections. Nonzero pullbacks on a modification have divisor order at least b_i(m) at each chosen prime divisor.",
      "Removing finitely many imposed divisorial orders gives an effective residual divisor and a global logarithmic quotient-metric potential w_m with Theta_m+dd^c w_m = [D_m]/m.",
      "The full integer trace limit established in Chapter18 applies along the unbounded selected section degrees and yields smooth convergence of the finite curvature combination Theta_m to Theta.",
      "One fixed Hermitian form dominates all smooth Theta_m. The normalized potentials lie in its PSH space, have a genuine L1 subsequential limit by Chapter1 compactness, and preserve positivity of the limiting closed current.",
      "The limiting closed positive current proves Bott-Chern pseudoeffectivity; Gauduchon pairing and Poincare-Lelong for each prime divisor yield precisely the finite trace inequality.",
      "A strict violation of one finite inequality forbids all sufficiently large nonzero restricted spaces: otherwise the selected unbounded degree sequence satisfies the theorem and contradicts that violation.",
      "The elliptic flat counterexample has irrational unitary multiplier, so every positive power has nontrivial monodromy. Any hypothetical degree-zero section is nowhere zero; its harmonic log norm is constant, and the entire lifted holomorphic function has constant modulus, contradicting nontrivial monodromy. This disproves a general converse, rather than stating an open question.",
      "For the projective-line weight (3/5) log(|z|^2/(1+|z|^2)), curvature is (2/5) beta+(3/5) delta_0. Integrability gives I(u)=O and I(2u)=O(-[0]); a section nonvanishing at zero therefore violates graded multiplication upon squaring.",
      "Bounded weighted sections form vector subspaces by the squared-norm sum bound, and multiplication of the bounded weighted densities makes their direct sum a graded algebra. Smooth metric changes preserve boundedness and finite volume gives inclusion into integrable sections.",
      "The retained SGPT almost-semigroup citation applies to a smooth projective variety with positive curvature on a big line bundle and positive multiplier-ideal volume. It does not assert integrable section spaces form a graded algebra.",
      "At finitely many logarithmic curve poles, k-mc>-1 is equivalent to k at least floor(mc), even at integral mc; boundedness requires k at least ceil(mc). Compactness gives the two exact twisted-bundle spaces.",
      "The positive curve curvature has atoms c_i and total degree ell. Their subtraction leaves a positive measure and d<=ell; the bounded local remainder has no polar mass, so the residual measure is exactly the extended measure of mass ell-d.",
      "If ell>d, both degrees are m(ell-d)+O(1); Riemann-Roch and Serre duality apply once degree exceeds 2g-2. If ell=d, both degrees stay bounded, and evaluation at k+1 distinct points bounds h0 by k+1 for nonnegative degree k. Negative degree gives no sections. Both normalized limits include r=0.",
      "On the scalar Hopf quotient, s shifts by -2 while alpha and zeta descend. The earlier Chapter8 identities alpha^n=0, zeta^2=0 and dd^c zeta=-alpha^2 give dd^c omega^(n-1)=0, fiber integral 2 and volume 2n with the same normalization.",
      "The Hopf bundle equivariance and metric factors cancel under contraction; its curvature is k alpha. Hartogs extension for n>=2 and Taylor-coefficient comparison force exact homogeneous degree km, including k=0.",
      "Unitary invariance of the Hilbert norm gives invariant kernel, and radial homogeneity cancels the metric factor. Transitivity on the covering space makes the kernel constant; its integral against the probability volume gives N_m=binomial(km+n-1,n-1).",
      "The Hopf degree equals k(n-1) times the fiber integral, hence 2k(n-1); growth at most m^(n-1) implies m^(-n)N_m tends to zero, while (k alpha)^n vanishes and the Hermitian volume equals 2n.",
      "The Chapter6 infinite-mass witness has trivial ideals at every positive weight. On the trivial bundle every weighted section space is one-dimensional and its basis potential is constant; all Bergman currents vanish although extended mass in the different Hermitian background is infinite.",
      "The closing transition supplies the bounded graded algebra for Chapter20, while quantitative kernel limits in later Chapter26 retain additional relations between selecting weight and line-bundle curvature; these later results are not independently reread here."
    ],
